 Okay, let's see. There are 36 penguins sunbathing. Then one-third of them jump into the ocean to swim. Another one-third go inside the cave to eat their dinner. The question is asking how many penguins are still left sunbathing.

First, I need to figure out how many penguins leave to swim and how many go to eat. Since one-third of the penguins go swimming, that would be 36 divided by 3. Let me calculate that: 36 divided by 3 is 12. So 12 penguins go swimming.

Then another one-third go to eat. That's also 36 divided by 3, which is another 12 penguins. So 12 go to eat.

So total penguins that left sunbathing are 12 plus 12, which is 24. Wait, but hold on, is that correct? Let me check again. The problem says "one-third of them jump in and swim" and "another one-third go inside the cave". So "them" refers to the original 36 penguins. So both groups are each one-third of the original 36. So yes, 12 each.

